finding.claim:
impute_score compares numerical cells by a ratio of pooled z-space RMSE and categorical cells by a ratio of 0/1 error (imputation_metrics.py:44-57). The headline comparisons inherit those two choices, and on this benchmark they are not robust. With the model and baselines held fixed and only the per-kind error changed, the project's verdict rule gives these counts (better/worse/no verdict over 21 variants, seeds 101/202/303, current defaults).

Against the 4-baseline bar of the report scripts:
- RMSE + accuracy: 15/2/4, with 17 variants below the bar on the mean;
- RMSE + macro-F1: 12/4/5, with 15 below the bar;
- MAE + macro-F1: 13/5/3.

Against a fixed hgb opponent, so the bar's identity cannot move: 18/1/2 drops to 13/4/4 under macro-F1. kr-vs-kp_80nan goes from better to worse (+0.071), as do credit-g_60nan and credit-g_80nan; credit-g_20nan and kr-vs-kp_60nan go from better to no verdict.

Against missForest, the external comparison of ADR 0016/0017:
- RMSE + accuracy: 14/1/6;
- MAE + accuracy: 9/6/6, with missForest newly better on biodeg_60nan, electricity, letter, pendigits and vehicle_60nan;
- MAE + macro-F1: 6/8/7.

So 'TRIDENT beats missForest' is a statement about squared error specifically, and the categorical wins at high missingness are accuracy bought by leaning on the majority class. Per-column induced cell counts are equal under the per-column MCAR injection (about 486-538 per column per fold on kr-vs-kp_80nan), so the equal per-column average in macro_f1_cat is not what drives the flip. Within-column rare-class noise remains a caveat.

RMSE is minimised by the conditional mean and 0/1 error by the conditional mode, so impute_score structurally prefers shrunken numbers and majority categories. This is the Shadbahr et al. 2023 failure mode, where MICE is worst by RMSE but best distributionally. The ledgers show the trained decoder does exactly this:
- credit-g_20nan: median per-column sd(imputed)/sd(truth) is 0.56-0.60;
- spambase_20nan: 0.49-0.53, against 0.98-0.99 on pendigits;
- credit-g_20nan: the majority category is predicted for 0.69-0.73 of cells, against a true share of 0.59;
- kr-vs-kp_80nan: 0.89-0.90 against 0.81, with 17-19 of 36 columns emitting one class only.

The pipeline's selection pressure points the same way:
- Optuna ranks trials on validation impute_score (ADR 0005/0008).
- ADR 0015 picks the decode checkpoint by validation-gap impute_score.
- The calibration diagnostic that 'won' on 13 variants shrinks numbers toward the training mean and falls categories back to the mode below a confidence threshold.

No distributional, dispersion or downstream metric exists anywhere in the code (grep for wasserstein/signed/bias returns nothing in src/training). The thesis plan nonetheless requires one.

finding.suggested_fix:
Add per population:
- a dispersion ratio per column;
- a per-column 1-D Wasserstein (numeric) and frequency total variation (categorical) between imputed and true values, averaged over columns;
- optionally a sliced-Wasserstein over full rows as in Shadbahr.

Also persist each baseline's imputed values beside the model's in the ledger so the same scores exist for the bar. Report these beside impute_score and never select on impute_score alone for thesis claims.

finding.claim:
The numerical ratio pools squared errors over all columns in z-space (imputation_metrics.py:48-52), so each column's weight is its share of the naive squared error. Under MCAR this share is roughly uniform on well-behaved tables. On heavy-tailed tables a handful of outlier cells set the denominator and most of the numerator:
- kc2_20nan: the pooled naive RMSE per fold ranges 0.55-2.11, and one column holds up to 50% of the naive SSE in a fold;
- biodeg_20nan: the range is 0.88-2.58, and one column holds up to 81%;
- by contrast pendigits ranges 0.99-1.01 and credit-g 0.94-1.10.

The store shows the same: kc2_20nan baseline/mean_mode/rmse_num_z has std 0.57 over 5 folds (min 0.58, max 1.95). The verdicts on these tables are therefore the verdicts on a few extreme cells. This explains two of the flips in the first finding: kc2_20nan is undecided under RMSE but the model is worse than knn5 under MAE (+0.054), and spambase_20nan is undecided under RMSE but the model is better under MAE (-0.048).

Several `_00nan` tables hold many repeated feature vectors, and random StratifiedKFold puts the copies on both sides of the train/test split:
- kc2: 186/522 rows are in a duplicated vector, 153 redundant copies, 6 groups with conflicting labels;
- spambase: 577/4601 rows, 3 conflicting groups;
- letter: 2177/20000 rows;
- biodeg: 6 rows; credit-g, kr-vs-kp, electricity, pendigits and vehicle: none.

On the seed-42 5-fold split, these test rows have an exact twin in the train fold: kc2 32.2% (25.7-39.4% per fold), spambase 10.8%, letter 8.8%, biodeg 0.2%. The same shares hold for induced test cells (32.0% / 10.7% / 8.9%).

A nan-euclidean kNN finds the twin at distance 0 and copies its value, so the baseline bar on these tables is partly memorisation. On twin cells vs the rest:
- kc2_20nan: knn5 0.114 vs 0.646, knn10 0.141 vs 0.671;
- kc2_60nan: knn10 0.315 vs 0.784;
- spambase_20nan: knn10 0.655 vs 0.975, and knn5 is worse than mean on non-twin cells (1.039);
- letter_20nan: knn10 0.261 vs 0.495.

The model gains far less from twins. In the stored kc2_20nan ledger it scores 0.682 / 0.259 on twin cells against knn5 0.131 / 0.124, and 0.672 / 0.561 on non-twin cells against knn5 0.740 / 0.762.

An Optuna trial's namespace never sets cv_folds, so it takes the predefined split. Under ADR 0008 it is ranked on the induced gaps of the predefined validation rows, which are deterministic cells of the variant. The confirmatory arms then run 5-fold CV over all rows, and every predefined validation row lands in a CV test fold.

For E39's five variants at seeds 101/202/303, the share of each CV test fold's induced cells that are objective cells is:
- credit-g_40nan: 9.7% (5.8-12.9%);
- kr-vs-kp_20nan: 10.0%;
- kr-vs-kp_40nan: 10.0%;
- spambase_20nan: 10.2%;
- spambase_40nan: 10.0%.

CONSOLIDATED relied on two dampeners for F-07-6/F-08-3: 'the CV run re-masks ... so no scored cell recurs' and 'the headline population is induced, which the search never touched'. Both held for the masked objective and are false for the induced one. The bias is one-directional. Arm T was selected on these cells; arm F's files were selected on the masked objective, and arm D was not selected at all.

C1 is still open at HEAD: StandardScaler is fit on the whole variant, test rows included, before the folds exist. The earlier review's 'impute_score is exactly / provably invariant' was verified per column. The numerical part of impute_score pools z-errors across columns, so each column is weighted by 1/sigma_c^2, with sigma_c fit on all rows. Heavy-tailed columns make that sigma depend on whether a test fold holds an outlier: max |sd_train/sd_all - 1| is 0.85 on kc2, 0.77 on biodeg_60nan, 0.63 on spambase_80nan, 0.32 on vehicle_60nan, 0.17 on electricity_20nan and 0.10 or less on credit-g. The kNN baselines also measure distance in these units.

kc2_20nan knn5, 5 folds, seed 42:
- pipeline: 0.6075;
- units only from the train fold: 0.6204;
- distances only: 0.6316;
- both: 0.6410 (+0.033 mean; per-fold maximum 0.087).

Other variants:
- knn10 on kc2_20nan: +0.032;
- kc2_60nan: +0.017 / +0.020;
- every other variant probed: |difference| <= 0.009.

The model's stored kc2_20nan ledger (2-fold) rescored in train-fold units moves 0.6724 -> 0.7007 on fold 1 and 0.5503 -> 0.5469 on fold 2.

The vocabulary half of C1 is negligible. Over 5 folds x 6 seeds, the induced test cells whose true category the train fold never shows number:
- 0 on credit-g_20nan, kr-vs-kp_20nan/_60nan and electricity;
- 1, 10 and 24 on credit-g_40/60/80nan;
- 6 on kr-vs-kp_40nan (B5's spcop 't');
- 32 on kr-vs-kp_80nan.

Each level is one rng draw (seed 42 + pct), so every confirmatory and replication study sees the same holes. The replication on seeds 101/202/303 re-draws the partition and the initialisation but never the mask. Draw-specific artefacts therefore recur in every replicate:
- the erased kr-vs-kp_40nan spcop 't';
- credit-g purpose 'retraining' nearly erased at 60/80nan (10 and 24 induced test cells with no training example);
- kr-vs-kp_80nan hdchk 't'.

The levels are independent draws: the share of 20nan holes also missing at 40/60/80nan is 0.385-0.404, 0.591-0.609 and 0.796-0.804. A within-table level difference therefore also carries draw-to-draw noise, with no replicate draw to estimate it. Paired arm differences on the same mask largely cancel this.

There is still no context-free share or row-bucket metric in src/training. Fully missing rows inside the 21 are:
- credit-g_80nan: 10 rows (1.0%);
- kr-vs-kp_80nan: 2 rows (0.06%);
- every other benchmark variant: 0, including electricity_20nan.

The 16.76% of rows in electricity_80nan that the review highlighted are outside the benchmark. ADR 0014 (gaps shown as [MASK] in the decode stage) also makes all-[MASK] rows less out of distribution. The issue returns if the ladder is completed: pendigits_80nan 2.96%, letter_80nan 2.73%, vehicle_80nan 1.54%, kc2_80nan 1.34%.

