finding.claim:
The fork's default classification numbers (0.858/0.799/0.742/0.661/0.531) and the paper's Table 3 (0.843/0.813/0.756/0.711/0.581) differ simultaneously on these axes:
- train share: 3-fold trains on 56.7% of rows, the paper on 80%;
- epochs: 300/150, while the authors' public code defaults to 40/40 and its Optuna samples 20-60 for both stages;
- tuning: untuned, against test-tuned;
- schedule: under cosine_legacy the end-of-pre-training learning rate flips between base and zero with the split (see cls-cosine-legacy-parity);
- test size: a third of the rows per fold, against 10%.
The only store run near the paper's setup (2026-08-04, commit dee4d6fd: predefined 80/10/10 split, 40/40 epochs, seed 42, untuned) averages 0.823 at 0%. The paper reports 0.843 and the 3-fold 300/150 sweep reports 0.858. So the ordering of fork and paper flips with the epoch budget and the split, and neither the +1.5-point gap at 0% nor the -5-point gap at 60/80% can be attributed to anything.

With T_max = epochs and one scheduler step per batch, the learning rate after training is base × (1+cos(πB))/2, where B = ceil(n_train/BATCH). It ends at the full base rate when B is even and at zero when B is odd, whatever the epoch count (verified with a torch probe: 300×2 gives 3.4e-4, 300×3 gives 0). Pre-training has no checkpoint, so the encoder handed to fine-tuning is that last state.

B parity depends on the split:
- predefined to 3-fold flips it on biodeg (4 to 3), credit-g (4 to 3), electricity (142 to 101) and vehicle (3 to 2);
- 5-fold flips it on biodeg, credit-g and kr-vs-kp (10 to 9);
- 10-fold keeps the predefined B on all 9 tables.
In an Optuna study BATCH is sampled from {64,128,256,512}, so parity becomes a hidden knob the test-scored search can exploit. B1 and ADR 0003 recorded the cycle count, not this end-state or its dependence on the split.
